<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William Phips</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1651">1651</a>–<a href="/years/1695">1695</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Sir William Phips</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sir William Phips was a Maine-born shipbuilder and treasure hunter who was knighted after recovering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> from a Spanish wreck off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a> in 1687. In 1690 he captured Port Royal in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acadia">Acadia</a> but failed in an attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a>. As the first royal governor of the Province of Massachusetts Bay from 1692, he set up the court for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salem-witch-trials">Salem witch trials</a> and later halted it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-phips.jpg" alt="William Phips" width="320" height="366" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gertrude Stein · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Sir_William_Phipps,_ca._1691.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
